Strategic financial plans convert long-term objectives into operating, investment, and funding needs.|Expansion requires factories, staff, working capital, and financing.|A revenue target alone is a complete financial strategy?|No|Yes|Only over five years
Annual operating plans translate strategy into year-specific targets and resources.|A ₹100 sales target is linked to volume, price, hiring, and cash needs.|Which improves the plan?|Explicit operational assumptions|Only a profit target|Only last year's totals
Master budgets connect departmental plans with consolidated statements and cash.|Sales planning affects production, procurement, labour, and funding.|Departments can budget independently without reconciliation?|No|Yes|Only in large firms
Incremental budgeting adjusts prior budgets; zero-based justifies spending anew; activity-based links resources to activities.|Support staffing is driven by forecast service tickets.|This most resembles…?|Activity-based budgeting|Pure incremental budgeting|Share valuation
Flexible budgets adjust for actual activity; rolling forecasts extend the planning horizon.|Actual volume rises 20%; variable-cost budgets should reflect that activity.|Compare actual variable cost only with the original fixed-volume budget?|No|Yes|Only at year-end
Driver forecasts link revenue, cost, headcount, and capex to operational assumptions.|100 employees × ₹10 compensation gives ₹1,000 before other costs.|A 10-person increase at the same rate adds…?|₹100|₹10|₹1,100
Variance analysis separates actual-versus-plan differences into meaningful drivers.|Actual units cost ₹6 versus ₹5 standard, for 100 units: price variance ₹100 adverse.|Is every adverse variance evidence of poor management?|No|Yes|Only for variable costs
Standard costing compares actual with expected unit costs; ABC allocates overhead using activity consumption.|Product A requires many setups despite low unit volume.|ABC may assign more…?|Setup-related overhead|Sales tax automatically|Equity capital
Responsibility centres distinguish cost, revenue, profit, and investment accountability.|A manager controls expenses but not sales or invested capital.|The most direct classification is…?|Cost centre|Investment centre|External stock exchange
Relevant costing includes future cash differences; sunk and unavoidable costs do not change a make-or-buy choice.|Supplier price ₹8 versus avoidable internal cost ₹6 per unit.|Ignoring other differences, choose…?|Make internally|Buy externally|Decide from sunk cost
Internal transfer prices shape divisional incentives; opportunity costs matter when capacity is constrained.|An internal sale displaces a profitable external sale.|Include…?|Foregone contribution|Only production wages|Only historic depreciation
Product mix should consider contribution per scarce resource, not just per unit.|A earns ₹10 using two hours; B earns ₹9 using one hour.|With scarce hours, B offers…?|Higher contribution per hour|Lower contribution per hour|Identical contribution per hour
KPI trees connect outcomes to operational drivers and distinguish leading from lagging measures.|Profit links to volume, price, contribution, and fixed costs.|A useful KPI tree should…?|Explain controllable drivers|Contain only final profit|Maximise metric count
Forecast accuracy evaluates errors, bias, horizon, and relevance; metrics require consistent definitions.|Forecast ₹100 and actual ₹80 gives ₹20 overforecast.|Repeated positive forecast-minus-actual errors suggest…?|Optimistic bias|Perfect accuracy|No pattern
Finance partnering translates analysis into decisions; board reporting highlights performance, risks, options, and actions.|A margin decline is explained with pricing and cost drivers plus corrective choices.|Best report emphasis?|Decision-relevant evidence and actions|All spreadsheet cells|Only a colourful chart
